WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.829 --> 00:00:09.400
There are thousands of science fiction stories
about robots who are “more human than human.”

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.559
But what is it that drives us to make machines
that look like us?

00:00:13.559 --> 00:00:15.730
It certainly isn’t easy.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:20.660
One of the hardest parts is giving humanoid
robots something that we humans take for granted,

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:23.810
our ability to walk on two legs.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:25.760
Robots with four legs are more stable,

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.000
because more legs means more points of contact

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:34.400
with the ground, and less engineering is needed to
make a workable robot.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:40.470
But two legs provide a sense of familiarity
that helps humans empathise with the machines.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:45.640
Teaching robots to walk used to
involve a lot of trial and error.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:52.180
Robots like ASIMO took several years of research
and engineering to stand and walk around.

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:58.229
With the advent of machine learning, freestanding
robots are learning to take their first steps

00:00:58.229 --> 00:01:01.760
by training on thousands of hours of simulations,

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.340
even before they have legs.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.470
So we can get them to stand up.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:10.250
But why on Earth do we need upright humanoid robots?

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.180
What can they do that other robots can’t?

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.040
It turns out, a whole lot.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:19.980
Researchers at the University of Tokyo are
trying to build a complete human analogue,

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.350
with a metal skeleton and hydraulic muscles.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:27.640
Their current models, Kenshiro and Kengoro,

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.960
can perform many of the same movements as humans,

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.400
including push-ups and sit-ups.

00:01:34.409 --> 00:01:39.860
Aside from making great bodybuilding coaches,
the researchers say they give us insight,

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:43.460
into the intricacies of human physiology.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:46.190
But there are some downsides.

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.940
One of those is psychological:

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:52.640
When robots start looking too “real,” they unsettle us,

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.940
causing us to trust them less.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:59.340
That hasn’t stopped scientists from tackling
the daunting task

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:02.900
to make robots look and act more human.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:12.010
In 2004, European researchers began work on
the iCub, a robot designed to mimic a child.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:17.540
iCub is the hardware that houses the artificial
intelligence software that powers it.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:23.470
The robot can observe its environment through
camera eyes and microphone ears,

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:28.010
touch sensors in the hands, and a “skin” made of printed
circuit capacitors.

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:35.820
The motivation for creating iCub is to give
AI not only a voice, but also a body.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:40.440
iCub has been trained to observe and respond
to its surroundings.

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:45.349
By watching humans, the robot has learned
to grasp and manipulate objects.

00:02:45.349 --> 00:02:52.080
Some of the project researchers have even
taught it to identify the objects it picks up.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:58.020
But one of iCubs most valuable lessons for
the field of robotics is in sociability.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:02.780
Researchers are training the robot to maintain
eye contact with humans,

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:06.320
determine their emotions through facial expressions and gestures,

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:08.780
and respond to nonverbal communication

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:11.040
in order to complete tasks.

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:17.490
This kind of research could mark the beginning
of a new era in human-robot interactions.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:20.270
But what about those science fiction dreams?

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:23.600
One day, we’ll ask the robots what they think.

